class GenericMember {
  definition {
    One member of a GenericHyperedge — a specific concept (species)
    plus ISO 704:2022 presence, count, and delimiting metadata.

    Mirrors HyperedgeMember shape exactly, plus the `characteristic`
    field (ISO 704:2022 §5.5.4.2.1 delimiting characteristic text)
    that distinguishes this species from coordinate concepts within
    the same rake.

    ISO 704:2022 MECE dimensions (inherited shape):
      presence      — required (default) | optional
      count         — exactly_one (default) | at_least_one | multiple
      is_delimiting — false (default) | true

    The combination (optional, at_least_one) is invalid.
  }
  +ref: <<StandardDocument>> ReferenceToTermbase [1] {
    definition {
      The specific concept — the species in a genus–species rake.
      Encoded as a ConceptRef (source + id, optional text).
    }
  }
  +presence: PartitivePresence [0..1] {
    definition {
      Whether this species must be present in every instance of the
      genus (required) or only in some (optional).

      ISO 704:2022 line style: solid (required) vs dashed (optional).
      Defaults to required.
    }
  }
  +count: PartitiveCount [0..1] {
    definition {
      How many instances of this species exist. Defaults to exactly_one.
      Invalid in combination with presence: optional + count: at_least_one.
    }
  }
  +is_delimiting: Boolean [0..1] {
    definition {
      ISO 704:2022 delimiting species. When true, this species
      distinguishes the genus from coordinate genera. Orthogonal to
      presence and count. Defaults to false.
    }
  }
  +characteristic: <<BasicDocument>> LocalizedString [0..1] {
    definition {
      ISO 704:2022 §5.5.4.2.1 delimiting characteristic text — the
      intension-difference that distinguishes this species from
      coordinate concepts within the same rake.

      Example (ISO 704:2022 §5.5.4.2.1 — computer mouse by movement
      detection):
        mechanical mouse:     "detecting movement by means of rollers"
        optomechanical mouse: "detecting movement by means of rollers and light sensors"
        optical mouse:        "detecting movement by means of light sensors"

      Without characteristic text, the rake is just a list of narrower
      concepts rather than a coordinated decomposition. The field is
      optional but recommended; some datasets only carry the criterion
      on the rake, not the characteristic on each species.

      Localized — carry text keyed by ISO 639 language code.
    }
  }
}

